  "image_name": "9442aad4e34085e0bc530bb13f7918c1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how coastal groundwater systems in three climate zones—high-latitude permafrost, temperate, and tropical—look today versus under climate change. Across all settings, the main message is that warming and sea-level rise alter the balance between fresh groundwater and seawater, changing both subsurface flow and coastal impacts.\n\nIn the present-day panels, freshwater generally flows seaward through the subsurface, with some freshwater discharge to the coast and a separate zone of saline groundwater near the shoreline. In the climate-change panels, this structure is disrupted. Sea-level rise pushes seawater farther inland and upward, increasing saltwater intrusion. Groundwater exchange appears more complex, with stronger lateral and vertical seawater intrusion pathways and changes in fresh and saline submarine groundwater discharge.\n\nEach environment shows distinct consequences. In permafrost terrain, thawing and active-layer thickening create taliks and enhance groundwater exchange. In temperate coasts, rising water tables and salinization affect wells and vegetation, including “ghost forests.” In tropical settings, higher water tables, localized flooding, wastewater leakage, coastal erosion, and reef degradation are highlighted. Overall, the figure shows that climate change intensifies coastal groundwater–seawater interactions and amplifies ecological and water-resource risks.",
